# assets/theme/theme.R: the portfolio's look in R: the color tokens, the Shiny apps' theme and the
# ggplot2 figure ink. Generated from assets/theme/tokens.css by build_pages.py; edit the tokens (or the
# app rules in build_pages.py), not this file. build_pages.py also writes everything below this header
# into apps/_shared/portfolio_app.R, which the apps source: Shinylive apps cannot read files outside
# their folder, and the Pages build copies that one file next to each app.R. The Quarto guides and
# snapshots source this file for figure ink (bp_ggplot_theme, one rendering per theme).
# ---- the portfolio theme for R (generated by build_pages.py from assets/theme/tokens.css) ----
#   page_sidebar(theme = bp_theme(), bp_theme_head(), title = div("Title", bp_theme_toggle()), ...)
#   renderPlot({ k <- bp_palette(input$bp_theme); plot(x, col = k$tone$red[["fill"]]) })
# bp_theme()         Bootstrap 5.3 as bslib ships it, precompiled: in the browser (webR) a theme with
#                    custom Sass variables is compiled at every start, so the colors come from the
#                    CSS variables of bp_theme_head() instead.
# bp_theme_head()    The theme choice the site makes: a saved choice (localStorage "bp-theme"), then
#                    the system setting, then dark; it follows later changes (the toggle in the
#                    site's top bar on the launch page, another tab, the system setting), sets
#                    data-bs-theme on <html> and reports it to R as input$bp_theme. With it come the
#                    tokens as CSS variables under the site's names (--bg, --surface, --ink, ...),
#                    the tones (--tone-red-fg, -bg, -line, -fill, -tile), Bootstrap's variables
#                    mapped onto them and the shared component rules.
# bp_theme_toggle()  The site's light and dark button, for a standalone run; it hides when the app
#                    is shown inside the site's launch page, whose top bar has the toggle.
# bp_palette(mode)   Plot colors for "light" or "dark" (anything else: dark): the tokens, and the
#                    tones as bp_palette(mode)$tone$red[["fill"]].
# bp_tones           Series colors: fg (text: 4.5:1 on its bg and on every surface), bg and line
#                    (a chip), fill (a mark or a bar: 3:1 on every surface) and tile (a cell behind
#                    ink text: 4.5:1). build_pages.py --check fails if a pair drops below its floor.
